# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  IFOS — earlyoom
#
#  Running out of memory on a 4 GiB machine does not produce an error message.
#  It produces a computer that stops answering for minutes while the kernel
#  thrashes the page cache, and frequently never comes back: the kernel's own
#  OOM killer only acts once there is no memory left at all, and by then the
#  desktop has been unusable for a long time. Every student who has said "meu
#  notebook travou" was describing this.
#
#  earlyoom watches the free-memory figure and kills the single worst offender
#  while the machine is still responding. The result is losing one browser tab
#  instead of losing the session.
#
#  Read by earlyoom.service as an EnvironmentFile. Note what that means for
#  quoting: systemd splits $EARLYOOM_ARGS at whitespace and does *not* remove
#  quotes, so a pattern written as '...' would reach earlyoom with the quote
#  characters still in it and match nothing at all, silently. Every pattern
#  below therefore contains no whitespace - the process names that do have a
#  space in them use `.` where the space goes.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

#  -m 6 -s 6   act at 6% free memory *and* 6% free swap, not at zero. Both have
#              to be low: zram makes swap real memory here, so a machine with
#              swap left is not actually out of anything yet.
#  -r 0        do not print a memory report every second. It writes to the
#              journal forever, on a laptop whose journal is already capped.
#  --avoid     never the session itself. Killing Xorg, sddm or i3 to free memory
#              logs the person out, which is the outcome this exists to prevent
#              - and it takes everything they had open with it.
#  --prefer    the things that are actually eating it and that are built to lose
#              a process: a browser reopens the tab, a compiler can be run
#              again. Named as they really appear in /proc/*/comm, which is
#              truncated to fifteen characters: "Isolated.Web.Co", not
#              "Isolated Web Content".
EARLYOOM_ARGS=-m 6 -s 6 -r 0 --avoid ^(systemd|Xorg|sddm|sddm-greeter|i3|polybar|dunst|pipewire|wireplumber|NetworkManager|sshd)$ --prefer ^(firefox|Web.Content|Isolated.Web.Co|chrome|chromium|steam|steamwebhelper|java|cc1plus|ld|rustc|node|code)$
